We first make the preliminary observation that the sequence is periodic  $\pmod{n}$  for any positive integer  $n$  with period  $n$ . Also if  $2\mid a_i$  for some term, then  $2 \mid a_{i+1}$  and  $2 \mid a_{i-1}$  since the average of any two consecutive terms is an integer. Inducting on this claim we can show that 2 is a factor of any term of the sequence.  Hence assume all terms are odd in what follows.

Now, say there exists some index  $i$  such that  $p \mid a_i$  for an odd prime  $p$ . Since  $1$  is a quadratic residue  $\pmod{p}$  and there exists quadratic non-residues  $\pmod{p}$  this implies that there exists positive integers  $m$  and  $m+1$  such that  $m$  is a quadratic residue  $\pmod{p}$  and  $m+1$  is a non-quadratic residue. Now, the given condition implies, 
\begin{align*}
    a_i+a_{i+1}+\dots + a_{p+i-1} & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
    a_{i+1}+ a_{i+2}+\dots + a_{p+i-1} & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
    a_{i+1}+ \dots + a_{i+p-m-1} & \equiv -\left(a_{i+p-m}+ \dots + a_{p+i-1}\right) \pmod{p}
    (p-m-1)A^2 & \equiv -mB^2 \pmod{p}
    (m+1)A^2 & \equiv mB^2 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
where the right-hand side is a quadratic residue  $\pmod{p}$  while the left-hand side is a non-quadratic residue unless  $p \mid A,B$ . But then, if  $p \equiv -1 \pmod{4}$  note, 
\begin{align*}
    a_{i+1} + a_{i+2}+\dots + a_{i+p-m-1} & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
    a_{i+1} & \equiv-(a_{i+2}+\dots +a_{i-p-m-1}) \pmod{p}
    &\equiv -mC^2 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
by the given condition which is a contradiction since  $-1$  is a non-quadratic residue  $\pmod{p}$ , unless  $p\mid C$  and hence,  $p\mid a_{i+1}$ . Thereafter, an easy induction shows that  $p\mid a_r$  for all  $r \ge i$  and since the sequence is periodic  $\pmod{p}$  with period  $p$  it follows that  $p \mid a_r$  for all  $r \ge 1$ . We may then divide out all our terms by  $p$  and repeat (since  $0<m,m+1<p$  dividing by  $p$  does not affect the integrality of the averages) until we either have  $\nu_p(a_i)=1$  for all terms  $a_i$  or  $\nu_p(a_i)$  for some subsequence which results in a contradiction.

Similarly if  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$  note, 
\begin{align*}
    a_{i+p-m}+a_{p+i-m+1}+\dots + a_{p+i-1} & \equiv 0 \pmod{p}
    a_{i+p-m-1} + a_{i+p-m}+a_{p+i-m+1}+\dots + a_{p+i-1}& \equiv a_{i+p-m-1} \pmod{p}
    (m+1)D^2 & \equiv a_{i+p-m-1} \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
which is a clear contradiction since  $(m+1)$  is a quadratic non-residue  $\pmod{p}$  unless  $p \mid D$  and hence  $p \mid a_{i+p-m-1}$ . Now note that since  $-(m+1) \not \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  ( $-1$  is a quadratic residue  $\pmod{p}$  if  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$ ) by a straightforward induction we have  $p \mid a_{i+k(p-m-1)}$  for all positive integers  $k$  which covers all residue classes  $\pmod{p}$  in the index. Again resorting to the periodicity  $\pmod{p}$  of the sequence it thus follows that  $p \mid a_r$  for all  $r \ge 1$ . We may then divide out all our terms by  $p$  and repeat (since  $0<m,m+1<p$  dividing by  $p$  does not affect the integrality of the averages) until we either have  $\nu_p(a_i)=1$  for all terms  $a_i$  or  $\nu_p(a_i)$  for some subsequence which results in a contradiction.

Hence, we must have  $\nu_p(a_i)$  is constant for each odd prime  $p$  dividing some term of the sequence. If the sequence consists of all even numbers, we must also have  $\nu_2(a_i)$  being constant since if there exists two consecutive terms for which  $\nu_2(a_i)=r \ne s = \nu_2(a_{i+1})$ , we have
\[2^{\max(r,s)-1}m(2^{\min(r,s)-1}+1) = \frac{2^rm+2^sm}{2} = x^2\]
which is a contradiction since  $\nu_2\left(2^{\max(r,s)-1}m(2^{\min(r,s)-1}+1)\right) = \max(r,s)-1$  which is odd since both  $r$  and  $s$  are even which is a contradiction. This implies the sequence is indeed constant as claimed.

