Note that along each line of vertices, the numbers repeat as  $1,2,3,1,2,3,\dots$ . $B$ 's strategy to prevent  $A$  from getting  $6$  in a row is to unmark any vertex labelled  $1$  whenever it is marked. To show that this works, consider the state right before  $A$  makes their last turn. They have to be in one of the following cases ( $X$  means  $A$  marks these vertices to make  $6$  in a row):

Case 1:  $1$   $2$   $3$   $1$   $X$   $X$ Case 2:  $1$   $2$   $3$   $X$   $X$   $3$ Case 3:  $1$   $2$   $X$   $X$   $2$   $3$ in each of these cases, all three numbers appear in the marked vertices before  $A$  marks the final two, which cannot happen if  $B$  keeps unmarking  $1$ . Thus,  $k=6$  is unwinnable.

We claim that the desired minimum is 16, and it holds when  $x_1=x_2=x_3=x_4=1$ , and  $a=c=-4, b=6, d=1$ . Of course,  $b-d=5$ .
 By Viette's formulae we get:  $$ x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4=-a $$    $$ \sum_{1\leq i<j\leq 4}x_ix_j =b $$    $$ \sum_{1\leq i<j<k\leq 4}x_ix_jx_k =-c $$    $$ x_1x_2x_3x_4=d $$  Denote   $$ s_1=x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2+x_4^2, ~~s_2=\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq 4}x_i^2x_j^2, ~~s_3=\sum_{1\leq i<j<k\leq 4}x_i^2x_j^2x_k^2, ~~s_4=x_1^2x_2^2x_3^3x_4^2 $$  Hence   $$ (x_1^2+1)(x_2^2+1)(x_3^2+1)(x_4^2+1)=1+s_1+s_2+s_3+s_4 $$  But   $$ s_1=\left(\sum_{i=1}^4 x_i\right)^2-2\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq 4} x_ix_j=a^2-2b $$    $$  s_2=\left(\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq 4} x_ix_j\right)^2-2\left(\sum_{cyc}(x_1^2x_2x_3+x_1^2x_3x_4+x_1^2x_2x_4+x_1x_2x_3x_4)-x_1x_2x_3x_4\right)=b^2-2ac+2d $$    $$  s_3=\left(\sum_{1\leq i<j<k\leq 4}x_ix_jx_k\right)^2-2x_1x_2x_3x_4\cdot \left(\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq 4} x_ix_j\right)=c^2-2bd $$    $$ s_4=d^2 $$  Therefore 
\begin{align*}
(x_1^2+1)(x_2^2+1)(x_3^2+1)(x_4^2+1) &=a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2-2bd-2ac-2b+2d+1  &=(a-c)^2+(b-d)(b-d-2)+1  &\geq 0+5\cdot (5-2) +1=16.
\end{align*}

