Let  $O$  be the circumcenter of triangle  $AHC$  and its circumcircle  $\omega$ . We reflect everything over line  $AC$  to get points  $B'$ ,  $P'$ ,  $O'$ ,  $X'$ , and  $Y'$ . It is a well known fact that the reflection of  $H$  over line  $AC$  is on the circumcircle of triangle  $ABC$  (We can get this by angle chasing) so  $B'$  must be on  $\omega$ . Also, the reflection of the orthocenter of triangle  $APC$  over line  $AC$  must be on the circumcircle of triangle  $APC$ , so  $Y'$  is on  $\omega$ . Since  $Y$  is the orthocenter of triangle  $APC$ ,  $\angle ACY' = \angle ACY = 90^\circ - \angle PAC = 90^\circ - \frac{A}{2}$ . Now, since  $O$  and  $X$  are on the perpendicular bisector of segment  $AP$ ,  $O'$  and  $X'$  are on the perpendicular bisector of segment  $AP'$ . Also, since  $X$  is on the perpendicular bisector of  $AB$ ,  $X'$  is on the perpendicular bisector of  $AB'$ .  $O$  is also on the perpendicular bisector of  $AB'$ . Also, line  $AC$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $O'O$ .
