Because of our perpendicular segments, we have  $\angle OO'X = 90^\circ - \angle O'RA = 90^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle CAP') = \frac{A}{2}$ . Since  $OX'$  is perpendicular to  $AB'$  and  $OO'$  is perpendicular to  $AC$ , we have a cyclic quadrilateral and  $\angle O'OX = 180^\circ - \angle B'AC = 180^\circ - A$ . Then  $\angle OXO' = 180^\circ - \angle OO'X - \angle O'OX = \frac{A}{2}$  so  $X'O = O'O$ . Now, we also have  $\angle Y'OA = 2\angle Y'CA = 2(90^\circ - \frac{A}{2}) = 180^\circ - A$ . Now consider a counterclockwise rotation about  $O$  by  $180^\circ - A$  degrees. This takes  $Y'$  to  $A$  and  $X'$  to  $O'$ . Since a rotation preserves lengths,  $XY = X'Y' = O'A$ . But  $O'$  is the circumcenter of triangle  $ABC$ , so we are done.

