Oftentimes, the key of AMC floor function problems are to visualize the graph.
Although the entire expression looks scary at first, the core idea is to break it down into what we know.
Let's look at  $x-\lfloor x\rfloor$ , the fractional part of  $x$ .
[asy]
import graph;

size(8cm);

real a = -1, b = 5;
int minN = floor(a), maxN = floor(b);

// Function for f(x) on [n, n+1)
real piece(real x, int n) {
  return x - n;
}


draw((minN, 0)--(maxN + 1, 0), black);
draw((0, -1)--(0, 3), black);


for(int n = minN; n <= maxN; ++n) {
  draw(graph(new real(real x) { return piece(x, n); }, n, n+0.999),
       fuchsia+linewidth(1));
}

for(int n = minN+1; n <= maxN; ++n) {
  real yLeft = piece(n, n - 1); // Value approaching from left
  real yRight = piece(n, n);    // Value on right

  filldraw(circle((n, yLeft), 0.05), invisible, royalblue);     // Open circle (left limit)
  dot((n, yRight), royalblue);           // Closed dot (right endpoint)
}

[/asy]
After understand the shape of  $x-\lfloor x\rfloor$ , understanding how  $2014^{x-\lfloor x\rfloor}$  looks like becomes simple. 
We know how Exponentials curves  $a^x$  look like, and we can split  $x-\lfloor x\rfloor$  into repeated graphs of  $y = x$  every interval of length  $1$ .
Imagine taking any  $x$  on the  $x-\lfloor x\rfloor$  and we wish to graph  $2014^x$ .
The graph of  $2014^{x-\lfloor x\rfloor}$  will look like a bunch of repeated  $2014^x$ 's every interval of length  $1$  
[asy]
