We see that the  $n^{th}$  section  $x \in [n, n + 1)$ , treating  $x = n$  as  $x = 0$ , ie shifting graph because we care about the length of interval rather than location, can be written as  $f(x) = \lfloor(x\rfloor)\cdot(2014^{x} - 1) = 1$  as we are finding intersection. the  $(2014^{x} - 1)$  comes from as in the graph of  $f(x)$   $2014^{x}$  would have at  $x = 0$ , a  $1$  as  $2014^0 = 1$ , rather than  $0$ , but in the graph we start at  $y = 0$ . 
So simpler, we are treating each section as a separate exponential function and simply finding the length of the interval such  $f(x) \le 1$   $$ 2014^x - 1 = \frac{1}{n} $$    $$ 2014^x = \frac{n + 1}{n} $$    $$ x = \log_{2014}\left(\frac{n+1}{n}\right) $$  And since  $x \in [1, 2014)$  we have the section  $1$ ,  $2$ ,  $\ldots, 2013$ Thus the answer is  $$ \log_{2014}\left(\frac{2}{1}\right) + \log_{2014}\left(\frac{3}{2}\right) + \cdots + \log_{2014}\left(\frac{2014}{2013}\right) $$    $$  = {\log_{2014}}(2014) = \boxed{1} $$

