By symmetry  $CI=8$  as well. Note that \[\angle BIC=(180-2\angle BCI)=(180-\angle BCA)>90^{\circ}\] since  $\angle BCA<90^{\circ}$  (the triangle is isosceles). Therefore,  $BC^{2}>BI^{2}+CI^{2}\Rightarrow BC^{2}>128\Rightarrow BC>11.31$ . Note also  $BC<16$  due to the Triangle Inequality. Therefore  $BC\in\{12, 13, 14, 15\}$ . Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ . We analyze right  $\triangle IBM$  and use the double angle formula for cosine four times:



- If  $BC=12$  then  $\cos\angle IBM=\displaystyle\frac{12\div2}{8}=\displaystyle\frac{3}{4}$  so  $\cos\angle ABC=\cos 2\angle IBM=2\cos^{2}\angle IBM-1=2\left(\displaystyle\frac{3}{4}\right)^{2}-1=\displaystyle\frac{1}{8}$ . Then  $AB=6\div\displaystyle\frac{1}{8}=48$ .
- If  $BC=13$  then  $\cos\angle IBM=\displaystyle\frac{13\div2}{8}=\displaystyle\frac{13}{16}$  so  $\cos\angle ABC=\cos 2\angle IBM=2\left(\displaystyle\frac{13}{16}\right)^{2}-1=\displaystyle\frac{41}{128}$ . Then  $AB=\displaystyle\frac{13}{2}\div\displaystyle\frac{41}{128}=\displaystyle\frac{832}{41}$  which is not an integer.
- If  $BC=14$  then  $\cos\angle IBM=\displaystyle\frac{14\div2}{8}=\displaystyle\frac{7}{8}$  so  $\cos\angle ABC=\cos 2\angle IBM=2\left(\displaystyle\frac{7}{8}\right)^{2}-1=\displaystyle\frac{17}{32}$ . Then  $AB=7\div\displaystyle\frac{17}{32}=\displaystyle\frac{224}{17}$  which is not an integer.
- If  $BC=15$  then  $\cos\angle IBM=\displaystyle\frac{15\div2}{8}=\displaystyle\frac{15}{16}$  so  $\cos\angle ABC=\cos 2\angle IBM=2\left(\displaystyle\frac{15}{16}\right)^{2}-1=\displaystyle\frac{97}{128}$ . Then  $AB=\displaystyle\frac{15}{2}\div\displaystyle\frac{97}{128}=\displaystyle\frac{960}{97}$  which is not an integer.


Only  $BC=12$  gives an integer value for the length of one of the two congruent sides. Thus the smallest (and only) possible perimeter of  $\triangle ABC$  is  $12+48+48=\boxed{108}$ .</details>

