The total number of subsets is just  $\binom{2015}{1000}$ . Also, the number of subsets whose smallest element is  $r$  is  $\binom{2015-r}{999}$  for  $1 \le r \le 1016$ , because  $r$  has to be in the subset, and we have to choose  $999$  of the  $2015-r$  elements that are greater than  $r$ . Therefore, the sum of all the smallest elements is
\[\sum_{r=1}^{1016} r\times \binom{2015-r}{999}\]
\[=1\times \binom{2014}{999}+2\times \binom{2013}{999}+3\times \binom{2012}{999}\cdots+1016\times \binom{999}{999},\] which can also be written as
\[\begin{array}{lr}
&\binom{2014}{999}+\binom{2013}{999}+\binom{2012}{999}+\cdots+\binom{999}{999} 
+&\binom{2013}{999}+\binom{2012}{999}+\cdots+\binom{999}{999}  
+&\binom{2012}{999}+\cdots+\binom{999}{999} 
\vdots & \vdots 
+&\binom{999}{999}  
\hline 
\end{array}\]

By the hockey stick identity, the first row sums to  $\binom{2015}{1000}$ , the second row sums to  $\binom{2014}{1000}$ , the third row sums to  $\binom{2013}{1000}$ , and so on, and the last row sums to  $\binom{1000}{1000}$ , so we just have to find the sum
\[\binom{2015}{1000}+\binom{2014}{1000}+\binom{2013}{1000}+\cdots+\binom{1000}{1000}.\]
Hey look, we can use the hockey stick identity again to get that it equals  $\binom{2016}{1001}$ . Now, we just have to find the mean, which is the sum over the number of subsets, or
\[\frac{\binom{2016}{1001}}{\binom{2015}{1000}}=\frac{\frac{2016!}{1015!\cdot1001!}}{\frac{2015!}{1015!\cdot1000!}},\]
which simplifies to  $\frac{2016}{1001}=\frac{288}{143}$ , so our final answer is  $288+143=\boxed{431}$ , and we are done.

Basically use a weighted averages argument to get that the answer is  $\frac{1}{\binom{2015}{1000}}\sum_{k = 1}^{2015}k\binom{2015-k}{999}$ . To evaluate the sum, use the following standard trick: write the sum with each value of  $k$  corresponding to a different row.
\begin{align*}
S &= \binom{2014}{999}
&+\binom{2013}{999} + \binom{2013}{999}
&+\binom{2012}{999} + \binom{2012}{999} + \binom{2012}{999}
& \vdots
\end{align*}
Now, add column by column. The first column is  $\binom{2014}{999} + \binom{2013}{999} + \dots + \binom{999}{999} = \binom{2015}{1000}$  by hockey stick. Similarly, the second column is  $\binom{2014}{1000}$  and so on. Thus, 
\[ S = \binom{2015}{1000} + \binom{2014}{1000} +\dots + \binom{1000}{1000} = \binom{2016}{1001}.\]
The desired average is  $\frac{\binom{2016}{1001}}{\binom{2015}{1000}} = \frac{\frac{2016!}{1015!1001!}}{\frac{2015!}{1000!1015!}} = \frac{2016\cdot 1015}{1002\cdot 1001} = \frac{2016}{1001} = \frac{288}{143}\to 288 + 143 = \boxed{431}$ .

