Set  $\angle ABC = \angle ADB = \alpha$  and  $\angle AEC = \angle ACB = \beta$ . Note that   $$ \alpha+\beta = \frac 12 \angle O_2O_1B + \frac 12 \angle O_1O_2C =\frac 12( 90^{\circ} + 90^{\circ}) = 90^{\circ}. $$   Observe further that  $\sin \angle AO_1B = \frac 45, \cos \angle AO_1B = -\frac 35$ , so we can compute   $$ \sin \alpha = \frac{2\sqrt 5}5, \cos \alpha = \frac{\sqrt 5}5 $$   with the half-angle formula.

Now, set  $\angle CAE = \gamma$ , such that  $\angle DAB = 90^{\circ} - \gamma$ . The area condition becomes   $$ \sin \alpha \cos \gamma \sin(90^\circ -\gamma+\alpha) = 16 \cos \alpha \sin \gamma \sin(90^{\circ} - \alpha+\gamma). $$   Because  $\sin(90^{\circ} - \gamma+\alpha) =\sin(90^{\circ}-\alpha+\gamma)$ , we can reduce this to   $$ 16 \tan \gamma = \tan \alpha \iff \tan \gamma = \frac 18. $$   Therefore,  $\sin \gamma = \frac 1{\sqrt{65}}$  and  $\cos \gamma = \frac 8{\sqrt{65}}$ . Therefore   $$ \sin \angle DBA = \frac 1{\sqrt{65}} \cdot \frac{2\sqrt 5}5 + \frac 8{\sqrt{65}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt 5}5 = \frac 2{\sqrt{13}}, $$   so   $$ [ABD] = \frac 8{\sqrt{65}} \cdot \frac 2{\sqrt 13} \cdot \frac 2{\sqrt 5} \cdot 2 = \frac{64}{65}, $$   so the answer is  $\boxed{129}$ .

