path arc = arc((2.5,4),1.5,0,90);
pair P = intersectionpoint(arc,(0,5)--(5,5));
pair Pp=rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*P, Ppp = rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*Pp, Pppp=rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*Ppp;
draw(P--Pp--Ppp--Pppp--cycle);
[/asy]

Note that the inscribed square divides the original square into four right triangles; let  $a$  and  $b$  denote the lengths of the legs of these triangles and let  $s=1$  be the side length of the shaded squares.  It is [well-known]("http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h480570p2691439") that  $s$  satisfies the relation  $\tfrac1s=\tfrac1a+\tfrac1b$ .  Rearranging and utilizing the fact that  $a+b=5$  gives \[\dfrac11=\dfrac1a+\dfrac1b=\dfrac{a+b}{ab}=\dfrac5{ab}\quad\implies\quad ab=5.\] Hence the total area of the four right triangles is  $2ab=10$  and the requested answer is  $25-10=\boxed{15}$ .</details>
<details><summary>Generalization</summary>[asy]
draw((0,0)--(0,5)--(5,5)--(5,0)--cycle);
draw((1,1)--(1,4)--(4,4)--(4,1)--cycle);
path arc = arc((2.5,4),1.5,0,90);
pair P = intersectionpoint(arc,(0,5)--(5,5));
pair Pp=rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*P, Ppp = rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*Pp, Pppp=rotate(90,(2.5,2.5))*Ppp;
draw(P--Pp--Ppp--Pppp--cycle,linetype("4 4"));
[/asy]

Suppose concentric squares with side lengths  $a$  and  $b$  (with  $a\geq b$ ) are placed with corresponding edges parallel to each other.  Construct a third square which is inscribed by the larger square but circumscribes the smaller (as shown above).  Then as long as such a square exists, its area is simply  $ab$ . (The AMC8 problem is the case where  $a=5$  and  $b=3$ .)</details>

