The area of each of the yellow triangles in the diagram above is half the area of each of the  $3$  x $1$  rectangles since the base and height are shared. The area of the four rectangles is  $3\cdot 1\cdot 4 = 12$ . Hence, the area of the yellow triangles combined is half,  $\frac{12}{2} = 6$ .

Then we know that the middle red square has side length 3, since  $5 - 1\cdot 2 = 3$ . Finding the area of the square,  $3^2 = 9$ .

Now, combining the area of the triangles and the middle square, we get the area of the largest square that fits.  $6+9 = \boxed{15}$ . Hence, our answer is 15.

