From largest to smallest, let the squares be  $\mathcal{A}$ ,  $\mathcal{B}$ , and  $\mathcal{C}$ . Since the edges of  $\mathcal{A}$  are parallel to those of  $\mathcal{C}$ , it follows that the edges of  $\mathcal{B}$  make the same angles with those of  $\mathcal{A}$  as they do with those of  $\mathcal{C}$ .

This means  $\mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{B} \sim \mathcal{B} \cup \mathcal{C}$ . (This means that the shape obtained by taking  $\mathcal{A}$  and  $\mathcal{B}$  together is similar to the one obtained by taking  $\mathcal{B}$  and  $\mathcal{C}$  together.) Since  $[\mathcal{A}] = 5^2$  and  $[\mathcal{C}] = 3^2$ , the desired area is  $[\mathcal{B}] = 5 \cdot 3 = 15$ .

