<details><summary>Solution</summary>I claim that  $\boxed{60}$  is the smallest desired  $n.$  

First, define a new sequence  $(b_n)_{n \ge 0}$  such that  $b_n \coloneq a_n+1.$  Then, we can rephrase the conditions as:


- $b_0$  is a positive integer that is at least  $2,$
- For each nonnegative integer  $i,$  we have either  $b_{i+1}=2b_i$  or  $b_{i+1}=2-\tfrac{2}{b_i+1},$
- $b_n=2015.$

Observe that if we use the  $b_{i+1}=2b_i$  recursive relation to go backwards when  $b_{i+1}$  is less than  $2,$  then  $b_i$  will be less than  $1,$  after which we can never get a positive integer value for  $b_0.$  Also, it is easy to see that we can never use the  $b_{i+1}=2-\tfrac{2}{b_{i+1}}$  recursive relation if  $b_{i+1}$  is greater than  $2.$  Hence, it follows that the values of  $b_{n-1}, b_{n-2}, \ldots$  are actually fixed. Now, I prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** For all nonnegative integers  $k,$    $$ b_{n-k}=\frac{2015}{r_k}, $$   where  $r_k$  is the remainder when  $2^k$  is divided by  $2015.$ *Proof.* I proceed with induction. 

The base case, when  $k=0,$  is trivial because we already know that  $b_n=2015.$  

Now, suppose that   $$ b_{n-i}=\frac{2015}{r_i}. $$   If  $2r_i<2015,$  then it is easy to see that   $$ b_{n-i-1}=\frac{2015}{r_{i+1}}. $$   Otherwise, we have
\begin{align*}
    b_{n-i} = 2-\frac{2}{b_{n-i-1}+1}&\implies \frac{2015}{r_i} = 2-\frac{2}{b_{n-i-1}+1} 
    &\implies \frac{2}{b_{n-i-1}+1}=\frac{2r_i-2015}{r_i} 
    &\implies b_{n-i-1}+1 = \frac{2r_i}{2r_i-2015} 
    &\implies b_{n-i-1} = \frac{2015}{2r_i-2015} 
    &\implies b_{n-i-1}=\frac{2015}{r_{i+1}},
\end{align*}
thus concluding the inductive step. 

Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, I have proven the claimed result.  $\square$  

Therefore, we wish to find the smallest  $k$  such that the remainder when  $2^k$  is divided by  $2015$  is divisible by  $2015.$  If it is some divisor  $d$  of  $2015$  greater than  $1,$  then we will get   $$ 2^k=2015k+d, $$   but this means that  $d$  must divide  $2^k,$  contradiction. Hence, we want to find the smallest  $k$  such that   $$ 2^k \equiv 1 \pmod{2015}. $$   First, note that the prime factorization of  $2015$  is  $5 \cdot 13 \cdot 31.$  Then, it is not difficult to find that   $$ \text{ord}_2(5)=4, \text{ord}_2(13)=12, \text{ord}_2(31)=5, $$   so we need to have   $$ 4 \mid k, 12 \mid k, 5 \mid k. $$   The smallest positive integer such that this is true is obviously  $k=60,$  concluding the proof.  $\square$</details>

