**PROOF**
For  $n=2$ , we do have
\[
\begin{aligned}
 LHS&=\frac{\sqrt{1-x_1}}{x_1}+\frac{\sqrt{1-x_2}}{x_2}
&=\frac{x_2\sqrt{1-x_1}+x_1\sqrt{1-x_2}}{x_1 x_2}
&\le\frac{\sqrt{(x_2^2+ x_1^2)((1-x_1)+ (1-x_2))}}{x_1 x_2}
&<\frac{\sqrt{(x_1+ x_2)(2-x_1-x_2)}}{x_1 x_2}
&\le\frac{\sqrt{1}}{x_1 x_2}
&=RHS
\end{aligned}
\]**Induction hypothesis.** Suppose that the inequality is true for  $n=k$ .
Then for  $n=k+1$ , we do have
\[
\begin{aligned}
 LHS&<\frac{\sqrt{k-1}}{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_k}+\frac{\sqrt{1-x_{k+1}}}{x_{k+1}}
&=\frac{x_{k+1}\sqrt{k-1}+x_1 x_2 \cdots x_k\sqrt{1-x_{k+1}}}{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_{k+1}}
&\le\frac{\sqrt{(x_{k+1}^2+(1-x_{k+1}))(k-1+(x_1 x_2 \cdots x_k)^2)}}{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_{k+1}}
&<\frac{\sqrt{(x_{k+1}+1-x_{k+1})k}}{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_{k+1}}
&=\frac{\sqrt{k}}{x_1 x_2 \cdots x_{k+1}}
&=RHS
\end{aligned}
\]**Q.E.D.**

