Let  $\mathcal{A}$  be an alphabet of  $26$  letters. For a word  $x \in \mathcal{A}^{26}$ , denote by  $w(x)$  its weight (as defined in the problem statement) and denote by  $T(x) \subset \mathcal{A}$  the set of letters appearing in  $x.$  Note that \[ w(x) = \frac{1}{27 - |T(x)|}. \] We assign a value  $v(S)$  to each subset  $S \subset \mathcal{A}$  such that \[ \sum_{S \supset T} v(S) = \frac{1}{27 - |T|} \qquad (\star) \] for all  $T \subset \mathcal{A}.$  It is easy to recursively find and verify the correct values to satisfy  $(\star)$ : \[ v(S) = \frac{(-1)^{|S|}}{27 - |S|}. \] The property  $(\star)$  enables a straightforward computation of the desired sum over all words  $x$  by naively summing over all subsets  $S$  containing  $T(x)$ : 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{x \in \mathcal{A}^{26}} w(x) &= \sum_{x \in \mathcal{A}^{26}} \sum_{S \supset T(x)} v(S) 
&= \sum_{S \subset \mathcal{A}} \sum_{x \in S^{26}} v(S) 
&= \sum_{S \subset \mathcal{A}} |S|^{26}v(S) 
&= \sum_{k = 0}^{26} \binom{26}{k}k^{26}\frac{(-1)^k}{27 - k} 
&= \frac{1}{27}\sum_{k = 0}^{26}(-1)^k\binom{27}{k}k^{26} 
&= \frac{1}{27}\left[27^{26} + \underbrace{\sum_{k = 0}^{27}(-1)^k\binom{27}{k}k^{26}}_{\text{vanishes}}\right].
\end{align*}
In the second line we swap the summations, noting that  $T(x) \subset S$  iff  $x \in S^{26}.$  In the fourth line we denote  $k = |S|.$  Finally, the term in braces in the last line vanishes because it is a  $27$ th finite difference of a polynomial of degree  $26.$  We conclude that \[ \sum_{x \in \mathcal{A}^{26}} w(x) = \frac{27^{26}}{27} = 3^{75}. \]

Ah, the idea to construct  $v$  is very nice.

It's worth noting for the sake of the fora that the function  $v$  can be derived without appealing to guesswork and induction (at least not directly).  Indeed, using Möbius inversion on the lattice of subsets of  $\mathcal A$  implies that
\begin{align*}
v(T) = \sum_{S\supset T}\frac{\mu(T,S)}{27 - |S|} &= \sum_{S\supset T}\frac{(-1)^{|S| - |T|}}{27 - |S|} = (-1)^{|T|}\sum_{k = 0}^{26 - |T|}\binom{26-|T|}k\frac{(-1)^k}{k+1}
&= (-1)^{|T|}\sum_{k=0}^{26 - |T|}\int_0^1\binom{26-|T|}{k}(-x)^{k}\,dx 
&= (-1)^{|T|}\int_0^1(1-x)^{26-|T|} = \frac{(-1)^{|T|}}{27-|T|}.
\end{align*}

