Let  $\phi$  be the inversion at  $H$  that swaps  $A$  and  $F$ . We see that  $\phi((AH))=BC$ , so  $\phi(Q)=\phi((AH)\cap(ABC))=(N)\cap BC=M$ , where  $(N)$  is the nine point circle, and  $N$  is the nine point center. Now, let  $G$  be on  $(N)$  so that  $MG\perp HM$ .

<u>Claim:</u>  $G=\phi(K)$ .

<u>Proof of Claim:</u>
\[\phi(K)=\phi((HQ)\cap(ABC))=\ell\cap(N)\]
where  $\ell=\phi((HQ))$  is the line through  $M$  perpendicular to  $HQ$ . But since  $H,Q,M$  are collinear, we see that  $MG\perp HM$  and  $M\in(N)$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ We see that the perpendicular to  $MG$  at  $M$  passes through  $H$ , the perpendicular bisector of  $MG$  passes through  $N$ , so by linearity, the perpendicular to  $MG$  at  $G$  passes through the reflection of  $H$  about  $N$ , or  $O$ , the circumcenter of  $(ABC)$ . But since  $MG\perp HM$  and  $AQ\perp HM$ , we have  $MG\parallel AQ$ , so in fact the perpendicular to  $GM$  at  $G$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $AQ$ . To summarize,  $AQ\parallel\ell=HM$ , and  $G$  is the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of  $AQ$  and  $\ell$ . This clearly implies that  $(AQG)$  is tangent to  $\ell$ . Inverting this, we see that
\[\phi((AQG))=(FMK)\]
is tangent to  $\phi(\ell)=(HKQ)$ , so  $(FMK)$  is tangent to  $(HKQ)$ , as desired.

