Consider a negative inversion  $\Phi$  taking the nine-point circle to  $(ABC)$ . Then, notice that  $\Phi(F) = A$  and  $\Phi(M) = Q$ . As a result, \[\Phi((KQH)) = K^*M \qquad \Phi((FKM)) = (AK^*Q).\] Thus, we wish to show that  $K^*M$  is tangent to  $(AK^*Q)$ . Now, we know that  $\angle QKH = \angle K^*MH = 90^\circ$ , and we know that  $AQ \perp QM$ , so as a result,  $AQ \parallel K^*M$ . Thus, we wish to show that  $K^*A = K^*Q$ . Now, notice that the midpoints of  $\overline{AH}$  and  $\overline{QH}$  both lie on the nine-point circle, and the midpoint of  $\overline{AH}$  is the antipode of  $M$ . Thus, the angle with  $K^*$ , the midpoint of  $\overline{AH}$ , and the midpoint of  $\overline{QH}$  form a right angle, and so  $K^*$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AQ}$  as required.  $\square$

Negative invert around  $H$  with radius  $\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\text{Pow}_{H}(ABC)}.$  Then, we see that  $A$  and  $F$  swap by the Reflecting the Orthocenter Lemma, and it is well-known that  $(ABC)$  swaps with the nine-point circle of  $\triangle{ABC}$ . Also, if we let  $Q^{*}$  denote the inverse of  $Q,$  then we have
\begin{align*}
    90^\circ &= \angle{HQA} 
    &= \angle{HFQ^{*}}.
\end{align*}
Therefore,  $Q^{*}$  lies on  $\overline{BC},$  lies on the nine-point circle, and is distinct from  $F,$  so it must be  $M.$  In other words,  $M$  and  $Q$  swap under the inversion. 

Now, let  $K^{*}$  be the inverse of  $K$  under this inversion. We know that  $(KQH)$  swaps with line  $MK^{*}$  and  $(FKM)$  swaps with  $(AQK^{*}),$  so we wish to show that  $MK^{*}$  is tangent to  $(AQK^{*}).$  

Firstly, note that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{HMK^{*}} &= \angle{HKQ} 
    &= 90^\circ 
    &= \angle{HQA}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, since  $Q, H,$  and  $M$  are collinear, we know that lines  $AQ$  and  $KM$  are parallel. Therefore, it suffices to show that  $K^{*}$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AQ}.$  

Let  $N$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{HA},$  and let  $L$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{HQ}.$  Then, note that  $L$  inverts to the intersection of ray  $HM$  past  $M$  with  $(ABC),$  and  $N$  inverts to the intersection of ray  $HF$  past  $F$  with  $(ABC).$  It results that  $N$  and  $L$  both lie on the nine-point circle of  $\triangle{ABC}.$  Angle-chasing within the nine-point circle, we get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{K^{*}NL} &= 180^\circ - \angle{LMK^{*}} 
    &= 180^\circ - \angle{HMK^{*}} 
    &= 180^\circ - \angle{HKQ} 
    &= 180^\circ - 90^\circ 
    &= 90^\circ.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we  $K^{*}N \parallel ML,$  so line  $K^{*}N$  passes through the midpoint of  $\overline{AQ}$  and is perpendicular to line  $AQ.$  This implies that  $K^{*}$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AQ},$  concluding the proof.  $\square$

