Define  $S_n = a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n$ .

The key claim is the following:**<span style="color:#351C75">Claim</span>** :  $a_k \ge \frac{k-1}{S_{k-1}}$  for all  $k \ge 2$ *Proof.* We proceed by induction. The base case of  $k = 2$  is immediate. The inductive step is as follows:
\begin{align*}
a_k &\ge \frac{k-1}{S_{k-1}}
a_k(S_{k-1}) + a_k^2 &\ge k-1 + a_k^2
a_k(S_k) &\ge k-1 + a_k^2
\frac{ka_k}{a_k^2 + k-1} &\ge \frac{k}{S_k}
\end{align*}
And we are done upon noting that  $a_{k+1} \ge \frac{ka_k}{a_k^2 + k-1}$   $\square$  

Now to finish,**<span style="color:#351C75">Claim</span>** :  $S_k \ge k$  for all  $k \ge 2$ .
*Proof.* Again, we proceed by induction. The base case of  $k = 2$  is immediate by AM-GM. We have that 
\begin{align*}
(S_k - (k))(S_k - 1) &\ge 0 \qquad \text{(Induction Hypothesis)}
\frac{S_k^2+k}{S_k} &\ge (k+1)
S_k + \frac{k}{S_k} &\ge (k+1)
S_k + a_{k+1} &\ge (k+1)
S_{k+1} &\ge (k+1)
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\square$ <details><summary>Remark</summary>Also, how was this originally a P5? The IMO jury is just too wise 4 me sometimes ...</details>

Rewrite the condition as   $$ \dfrac{a_{k+1}}{k} \ge \dfrac{a_k}{a_k^2 + k - 1} \implies \boxed{\frac{k}{a_{k+1}} \le a_k + \frac{k-1}{a_k}} $$  Now sum cyclically to obtain  $\dfrac{k}{a_{k+1}} \le  a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_k \implies a_{k+1} \ge \dfrac{k}{ a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_k}.$ (**Note**that this holds for all  $k$ .)
Now induct on  $n$ . Base case when  $n=2$  is true because  $a_2 \ge \dfrac 1a_1 \implies a_1 + a_2 \ge a_1 + \dfrac 1a_1 \ge 2$ .
Now assume for some  $n$ ,  $ a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n \ge n$  holds.
\begin{align*}
 a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n + a_{n+1} &=  (a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n) + a_{n+1} 
&\ge ( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n) + \dfrac{n}{ a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n} \ \text{(from above result)} \cdots \star 
&\ge n + 1 
&\iff ( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n)^2 - n( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n) + n - ( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n) \ge 0 
&\iff ( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n - n)( a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n - 1) \ge 0 
&\text{Which is true from our hypothesis.}
\end{align*}
<details><summary>PS</summary>Notice that if we substitute  $ a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n \ge n$  in the starred step, we will get the reversed inequality and the problem doesn't get solved, this is by far the trickiest part of the problem ig.</details>

Inducting on  $n$  suffices. Take  $k = 1$  to get  $a_1a_1 \ge 1$ . Now using the AM-GM Inequality we get \[ a_1  +a_2 \ge 2\] This goves us the  $n = 2$  case. We can now rewrite the condition as \[ \frac{k}{a_{k+1}} \le a_k + \frac{k-1}{a_k} \] Now if we sum from  $k = 1$  to  $99$  we get the following \begin{align*} \sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_i &\ge \frac{n}{a_{n+1}}  
\implies a_{n+1} &= \frac{n}{\sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_i}
\end{align*} Hence we get the following \[ \sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_{i+1} \ge \sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_i + \frac{n}{\sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_i} \ge n+1\] and the last step follows from the fact that \[\sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_i \ge n\]

Flipping the inequality shows that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a_{k+1}} &\leq \frac{a_k^2 + (k-1)}{ka_k}  
\implies \frac{k}{a_{k+1}} &\leq a_k + \frac{k-1}{a_k}  
\implies a_k &\geq \frac{k}{a_{k+1}} - \frac{k-1}{a_k}  
\implies a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n &\geq \frac{n}{a_{n+1}}
\end{align*}
Now, we induct, with base case  $n = 2$  obvious from AM-GM. Since  $a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n \geq n$ , then
\[ a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n + a_{n+1} \geq (a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n) + \frac{n}{a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n} \geq n+1 \] 
because for some  $0 < e < 1$ ,
\begin{align*}
e + \frac{n}{n+e} &> 1 
\iff en + e^2 + n &> n+e 
\iff en + e^2 &> e 
\iff n + e &> 1 
\end{align*}
which is obviously true.  $\blacksquare$

