From  $P(f(x),x)$ , we have:  $$ f(0)=c=f(f(f(x)))-f(x)-1 \implies f^3(x)=f(x)+c+1\qquad (1). $$  Also, from  $P(x,0)$  we get:  $$ f(x-c)=f(f(x))-c-1 \implies f^2(x)=f(x-c)+c+1\qquad (2). $$  Now substituting  $x \to f(x)$  in  $(2)$ , and comparing that with  $(1)$ , we obtain  $f(f(x)-c)=f(x)$ . This implies that for  $x \in \text{Img}(f)$ , we have  $f(x-c)=x$ . Also, we have that if  $x \in \text{Img}(f)$ , then from  $(1)$  we obtain  $f(f(x))=x+c+1$ , hence  $x+c+1 \in \text{Img}(f)$ . As  $f(0)=c$ , we have  $c \in \text{Img}(f)$ , hence  $2c+1,3c+2,\ldots \in \text{Img}(f)$ . In particular, from  $3c+2 \in \text{Img}(f)$ , we have  $f(2c+2)=3c+2$ .
Now note that by  $P(x,f(x))$ , we get:  $$ f(x-f(f(x))=-1, $$  hence  $-1 \in \text{Img}(f)$ . Suppose  $f(a)=-1$ . Then from  $P(x,a)$ , we obtain:  $$ f(x+1)=f(f(x))-(-1)+1=f(f(x))\qquad (3). $$  Now note that  $f(f(f(x)))=f(f(x+1)=f(x+2)$ . Applying this to  $(1)$ , we derive that:  $$ f(x+2)=f(x)+c+1 \qquad (4). $$  Using  $f(0)=c$  and induction, this yields that  $f(2x)=x(c+1)+c$ . In particular,  $f(2c+2)=f(2(c+1))=(c+1)^2+c$ . But we also have  $f(2c+2)=3c+2$ , hence  $(c+1)^2+c=3c+2$ . Solving this quadratic yields  $c \in \{-1,1\}$ . We will now consider the following two cases based on the value of  $c$ :**Case 1:**  $c=1$ . Then  $f(x+2)=f(x)+2$ , hence from  $f(0)=1$  we have  $f(2x)=2x+1$  for all integers  $x$ . We also have  $f(2x+1)=2x+k$  for some integer  $k$ . Using  $(3)$ , we have:  $$ f(f(2x+1))=f(2x+2)=2x+3, $$  but using  $f(2x+1)=2x+k$ , we get  $f(f(2x+1))=f(2x+k)$ . If  $k$  is even, then  $f(2x+k)=2x+k+1$ . If  $k$  is odd, then  $f(2x+k)=2x+2k-1$ . Either way, equating both values of  $f(f(2x+1))$  gives  $k=2$ , hence  $f(2x+1)=2x+2$  and thus  $f(x)=x+1$  for all  $x$ , which yields one of the desired solutions.**Case 2:**  $c=-1$ . Then  $f(x+2)=f(x)$ , hence  $f$  is constant over evens and also odds. As  $f(0)=-1$ , we have:  $$ f(x)=\begin{cases}
