From  $(2)$ , it is clear that  $\exists a \in \mathbb{Z}$  s.t  $f(a) = -1.$  Then  $P(x,a)$  yields  $f(x + 1) = f^2(x).$  Substituting into  $(1)$ ,  $f(y + 2) = f(y) + f(0) + 1$ , hence  $f$  is linear on each quotient mod 2 with the same 'slope'. By inspection, the only linear functions that satisfy the assertion are  $-1, x + 1.$  The slopes of the two options are distinct, hence we conclude that on all integers,  $f(x) = -1$  or  $f(x)= x+1.$

