Now, FTSOC, assume every town  $A_i$  can be swept by some other town  $\sigma(A_i)$ . Consider the series  $A_i, \sigma(A_i), \sigma^2(A_i), \cdots$ . The series can not cycle as  $A_i$  can not sweep any other town in the series, but  $n$  is finite, contradiction.

Base case:  $n=1$ : trivial.

Let  $T_k$  be the invincible town for  $T_1$  to  $T_n$ . In particular, all right bulldozers of  $T_1$  through  $T_{k-1}$  cannot move past  $T_k$ . Split into two cases:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If the left bulldozer of  $T_{n+1}$  can sweep away all the right bulldozers between  $T_k$  and  $T_{n+1}$ , it follows that  $T_{n+1}$  is invincible.
    \item In the other case, take the largest right bulldozer  $R$  to the right of  $T_k$ , bigger than the left bulldozer of  $T_n$ . Now, all towns to right of  $T_k$  are not invincible, so it follows that  $R$  can sweep away everything up to  $T_n$ , and then  $T_{n+1}$  by hypothesis.
\end{itemize}

