As  $x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{n+1}$  are all integers, we get \begin{align*} m &\equiv n \pmod{n+1}
 m &\equiv n \pmod{n+2}
 &\ \vdots
 m &\equiv n \pmod{2n+1} \end{align*}
Then  $m \equiv n \pmod{ \text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1)}$ . Thus, we let \[ m = \text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) \cdot e +n. \] Since  $m>n$ , we get  $e>0$ . Using that, we arrive at \[ x_1x_2\dots x_{n+1}-1 = \left(\prod_{k=1}^{n+1} \frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1)  \cdot e}{n+k}+1 \right) -1 \] Now it is easy to show that there is exactly one power of two in the interval  $[n+1,2n+1]$ . Hence, let  $n+i = 2^{\ell}$  with  $1 \leq i \leq n+1$ . Then \[ v_2 \left(\frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+i} \right)< v_2 \left(\frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+j} \right) \] for any  $j \neq i, 1 \leq j \leq n+1$ . But then \[ \frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+j} \cdot e +1 \equiv 1 \pmod {2^{v_2 \left(\frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+i} \cdot e \right)+1}} \] for all  $j \neq i$ . Assuming  $x_1x_2 \dots x_{n+1}-1$  was a power of two after all for the sake of contradiction, we arrive at \[ \frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+i} \cdot e+1 \equiv 1 \pmod {2^{v_2 \left(\frac{\text{lcm}(n+1,n+2,\dots,2n+1) }{n+i} \cdot e \right)+1}}. \] That's cleary a contradiction for  $e>0$ . So we are done.  $\hfill \square$

We assume the contrary and consider a pair of positive integers  $m>n$  such that the given expressions is a power of two. Let  $2^t$  be the largest power of two strictly less than  $n+1$ , our key claim is as follows.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :** For such a pair of positive integers  $m,n$  we must have, </span>
    \[\frac{m+1}{n+1} \cdot \frac{m+2}{n+2} \dots \frac{m+n+1}{2n+1} -1 = 2^{\nu_2(m-n)-(t+1)}\]

*Proof :* Clearly,  $2^t \ge \left \lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right \rfloor +1$  by definition. Thus, 
    \[2n+1> 2n \ge 2^{t+1} \ge 2\left(\left \lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right \rfloor+1\right) \ge 2\left( \frac{n}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\right) = n+1\]
    which is within the range  $n+k$  for  $1\le k \le n+1$ . However, 
    \[2^{t+2} \ge 4\left(\left \lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right \rfloor+1\right) \ge 4\left( \frac{n}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\right) =2n+2>2n+1\]
    which is out of bounds. Thus, the only power of two which is in the range is  $2^{t+1}$  and the only multiple of  $2^{t+1}$  within the range is also itself. This means that 
    \[\min\left \{\nu_2\left(\frac{m-n}{n+k}\right) \right \} =  \nu_2(m-n) - \max\{\nu_2(n+k)\} = \nu_2(m-n) - \nu_2(2^{t+1}) = \nu_2(m-n)-(t+1)\]
    for all  $1 \le k \le n+1$  which is unique. However this means,  $\nu_2\left(\frac{m-n}{n+k}\right) >  \nu_2(m-n)-(t+1)=X$  for all  $n+k \ne 2^{t+1}$  which allows us to note that, 
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{m+1}{n+1} \cdot \frac{m+2}{n+2} \dots \frac{m+n+1}{2n+1}-1 & = \left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+1}\right)\left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+2}\right) \cdots \left(1+\frac{m-n}{2n+1}\right)-1 
        & \equiv (1)(1) \dots (1)-1 \pmod{2^{X}} 
        & \equiv 0 \pmod{2^X}
    \end{align*}
    However, 
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{m+1}{n+1} \cdot \frac{m+2}{n+2} \dots \frac{m+n+1}{2n+1}-1 & = \left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+1}\right)\left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+2}\right) \cdots \left(1+\frac{m-n}{2n+1}\right)-1 
        & \equiv (1+2^X)(1) \dots (1)-1 \pmod{2^{X+1}} 
        & \equiv 2^X \pmod{2^{X+1}}
    \end{align*}
    due to the uniqueness of the minimum term noted above. Hence we conclude that, 
    \[\frac{m+1}{n+1} \cdot \frac{m+2}{n+2} \dots \frac{m+n+1}{2n+1}-1= 2^{\nu_2(m-n)-(t+1)} \]
    as claimed.

But now, note that if  $\nu_2(m-n)=T$ ,  $m-n \ge 2^T$ . Thus, 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{m+1}{n+1} \cdot \frac{m+2}{n+2} \dots \frac{m+n+1}{2n+1}-1 & = \left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+1}\right)\left(1+\frac{m-n}{n+2}\right) \cdots \left(1+\frac{m-n}{2n+1}\right)-1 
    & > 1+\frac{m-n}{2^{t+1}}
    & \ge 1+ \frac{2^T}{2^{t+1}}
    & > 2^{\nu_2(m-n)-(t+1)}
\end{align*}
which is a very clear contradiction. Thus, we conclude that it is indeed impossible for the described expression to be a perfect power of two which finishes the proof.

