Let  $n$  be a fixed integer with  $n \ge 2$ . We say that two polynomials  $P$  and  $Q$  with real coefficients are *block-similar* if for each  $i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n\}$  the sequences

\begin{eqnarray*}
P(2015i), P(2015i - 1), \ldots, P(2015i - 2014) & \text{and}
Q(2015i), Q(2015i - 1), \ldots, Q(2015i - 2014)
\end{eqnarray*}

are permutations of each other.

(a) Prove that there exist distinct block-similar polynomials of degree  $n + 1$ .
(b) Prove that there do not exist distinct block-similar polynomials of degree  $n$ .

*Proposed by David Arthur, Canada*

candidates:
**ANSWER:**  $B$  wins the game in all cases except for  $n=1,2,4,6$ , in which case it is a draw.**SOLUTION:** For notation's sake, we think of the game as one in which  $A$  and  $B$  are supposed to form a binary string of length  $n$ , with  $A$  putting zeros and  $B$  putting ones in the  $n$  squares available, such that  $A$  starts the game. The only restriction is that no two consecutive digits can be the same. The person who is no longer able to make a move loses the game. At any moment of time in the game, we call a square *accessible* to a player if he/she can make their move on that square without violating the rules. Now, we make two cases as follows-

<details><summary>CASE 1</summary><u>$n$  is odd</u>

For  $n=1$ , the game is obviously a draw. So from now on assume that  $n \geq 3$ . We provide an algorithm (as given below) which will help  $B$  win.



- Whenever possible,  $B$  writes  $1$  in the square symmetric (about the middle square) to that chosen by  $A$  in the previous move. $\text{ }$
- If at some point of time,  $A$  chooses the middle square. Then  $B$  will write  $1$  in the square adjacent to the middle square which is accessible to him (if both the adjacent squares are accessible, then choose one at arbitrary). WLOG assume that  $B$  chose the square to the left of the middle one. Then, if after some move of  $A$ , the symmetric square is not accessible to  $B$ , then  $B$  will choose the square to the left of the one chosen by  $A$ . Otherwise, go to Step 1.[/list]
Now, we show that this indeed works. First let's try to find out the situations in which we will be forced to apply the second step. Note that,  $B$  will always be able to find the symmetric square as long as  $A$  does not choose the middle square. Now, suppose  $A$  chooses the middle square. There are two possibilities as mentioned below. 

[list=1]
- Suppose that  $B$  is forced to choose the square on the right. Then the situation will look as shown in the figure (we have shown it for the 11 squares in the middle; we will always do so from now on).
