Here we have shown how  $A$  may force  $B$  to use Step 2 repeatedly. [/list]
In summary, we can say that  $B$  will always be able to perform a move if  $A$  is able to do so (This means that if  $B$  does not win, then the game must end in a draw). As there are an odd number of squares, so the last move will be of  $A$ . If suppose  $A$  is able to fill the empty square left, then in the next chance,  $B$  must also have a move, which contradicts the fact that all the squares have been used up. Thus, in the last chance,  $A$  will be unable to fill the square, and so  $B$  wins if  $n \geq 3$  is an odd number.</details>

<details><summary>CASE 2</summary><u>$n$  is even</u>

For  $n=2,4,6$  one can easily check by hand that the game is a draw. So from now on assume that  $n \geq 8$ . Suppose that  $A$  makes his/her first move at the  $(k+1)^{\text{th}}$  square, i.e. there are  $k$  squares before  $A$ 's chosen square. WLOG assume that  $k$  is even (If  $k$  is odd, then simply flip the board over). We make two cases.

[list]
- Suppose  $0<k<n$  (i.e.  $k \neq 0$ ). In that case, for the first move of  $B$ , we choose the first square. Consider the first  $k+1$  squares. They form a short version of the game with  $k+1$  squares (i.e. an odd number of squares) with the symmetric ends marked alternately with a one and zero. However, we have already shown that  $B$  always wins in a game having an odd number of squares and with similar conditions. Similarly, the remaining part (i.e. from square numbered  $k+2$  to  $n$ ) also forms a similar game with an odd number of squares, and so  $B$  can never lose here (it may be a draw in this sub-game if  $k=n-2$ ). Combining these two mini-games, we see that  $B$  must win overall too. $\text{ }$
- Now, we look at  $k=0$ , i.e. if  $A$  marks the first square. We make  $B$  choose the last square for the first chance. Suppose in the second move,  $A$  chooses the  $(m+1)^{\text{th}}$  square ( $m$  must be greater than  $1$ , as the first square has already been marked by  $A$ ). Again we divide this situation into two sub-parts. $\text{ }$ [list=1]
- Suppose  $m$  is odd. Then  $B$  chooses the  $m^{\text{th}}$  square for his/her second chance. Now the board is divided into two halves- Square  $1$  to  $m$  (a sub-game on an odd number of squares), and squares  $m+1$  to  $n$  (another sub-game with an odd number of squares). In both these sub-games, the situation is similar to our algorithm in Case-1 (only symmetric squares have been marked by  $B$ ), and so  $B$  can win in both places. Thus,  $B$  wins overall too. $\text{ }$
- Now take the case when  $m$  is even. We proceed by strong induction on even  $n \geq 8$ . For  $n=8,10,12$ , one can easily show that  $B$  can win when  $k=0$  and  $m$  is even. So from now on assume that  $B$  can win for all values  $8,10, \dots ,n-2$  (and assume  $n>12$ ). We make  $B$  choose the  $m^{\text{th}}$  square, and consider the two divisions of the board- Squares  $1$  to  $m$  and squares  $m+1$  to  $n$ . Both of these are sub-games with an even number of squares, and with the terminal squares containing different numbers. As  $n>12$ , atleast one of these two sub-games contains atleast  $8$  squares. By our induction hypothesis,  $B$  can win in this sub-game, and atleast draw the other sub-game. Thus,  $B$  will be able to win overall too.[/list]
</details>

Summarizing,  $B$  wins in all possible cases. Hence, done.  $\blacksquare$ **REMARK 1:** To be honest, even though the writeup to the case where  $n$  is even looks extremely small, it took me almost 1.5 times the time it took me to do Case-1. The construction for odd  $n$  is easy to get if one plays with some small cases. The main difficulty in Case-2 is to understand that it follows from Case-1 easily. Most of my time initially went in trying to find some construction for even  $n$ . It was only after failing for around an hour or so, that I realized my folly :P.**REMARK 2:** Another thing that perplexed me throughout the problem was why  $B$  fails to win when  $n=2,4,6$ , but wins for other even values of  $n$ . It was only at the end that I realised why this was happening. When I was playing around with small cases and saw that the game was a draw for  $n=2,4,6$  and a win for  $B$  if  $n=3,5,7$ , I was about to make my mind that  $B$  can win if  $n$  is odd, and there is a draw otherwise. It was sheer luck that I tried  $n=8$  just to get some more insight into the problem, and saw that  $B$  was able to pull a win. If that had not happened, then I probably would have spent around  $4$  hours on this one problem :P.

