<details><summary>The rest of the solution</summary>Return to the problem, we use strong induction on  $n$ , the number of sides of  $\Pi$ . The base case  $n = 3, 4$  are clear, so assume  $n\ge 5$ . Now, choose a side  $AB$  of  $\Pi$ , and consider two distinct Thaiangulations  $\mathcal{T}_1$  (blue),  $\mathcal{T}_2$  (red). Suppose the thaiangle containing  $AB$  in  $\mathcal{T}_1$  is  $\triangle ABC$ , and the thaiangle containing  $AB$  in  $\mathcal{T}_2$  is  $\triangle ABC'$ . If  $C = C'$ , then we delete the thaiangle in both Thaiangulations and apply induction hypothesis on each piece of the rest of the polygon (which contains one piece or two pieces). Lemma 2 then tells us that  $\mathcal{T}_1$  and  $\mathcal{T}_2$  can differ by two thiangles in only one of the pieces, so we're done. 

