The answer is all  $k \ge 2$ . **Necessity:** Assume there exists some function  $f$  for which  $f(m)+n$  and  $f(n)+m$  are always relatively prime. We show, via parity, that this is impossible. By selecting  $m$  and  $n$  odd, we get that there is at most one odd value in  $\mathcal{S} = \{f(1), f(3), f(5), \dots \}$ . However, by analogous logic, there is at most even value in  $\mathcal{T} = \{f(2), f(4), f(6), \dots \}$ . Selecting an even value from  $\mathcal{S}$  and an odd value from  $\mathcal{T}$  gives the desired contradiction.  $\square$ **Sufficiency:** We present an inductive construction, with our base case being  $f(1) = 2$ . Now, to define  $f(n)$ , we first let  $g(n)$  be a strictly increasing function such that \begin{align*} g(n) > \max_{m < n} (f(m)+n) \end{align*}
Now, we define  $f(n) = 2^{g(n) ! + 1} - n - 1$ . Fix  $m$  and pick some odd prime  $p \mid f(m) + n$ . Note that \begin{align*} f(m) + n \equiv 2^{g(m)! + 1} + n - m - 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}\end{align*} 
Therefore, we have that \begin{align*} f(n) + m \equiv 2^{g(n)!+1} + m - n - 1 \equiv m - n + 1 \pmod{p} \end{align*}
with the last equivalence following from Fermat's Little Theorem. This implies that  $p \mid f(n) + m$  if and only if  $p \mid 2^{g(m)!+1}$ , contradiction. It is trivial to check that four cannot divide both terms as well, so the function is good as desired.  $\square$

