Got a slightly different construction for  $n$  even, but overall this wasn't very hard.

If  $n$  is odd, then simply take  $k = n^z$  so that  $\frac{n^k}{k} = n^{n^z-z}$  is an odd integer, thus making its floor an odd integer as well. Henceforth assume  $n$  is even.

Consider using a number of the form  $k = p \cdot n^z$  where  $z$  is a positive integer and  $p \nmid n$  is a prime to be chosen later. Then
\begin{align*}
\frac{n^k}{k} &= \frac{n^{p \cdot n^z - z}}{p} 
&= \frac{n^{p \cdot n^z - z} - n^{n^z - z}}{p} + \frac{n^{n^z-z}}{p} 
&= n^{n^z - z} \cdot \frac{n^{(p-1) \cdot n^z} - 1}{p} + \frac{n^{n^z-z}}{p}.
\end{align*}
Note that  $n^{n^z-z}$  is even and  $\frac{n^{(p-1) \cdot n^z} - 1}{p}$  is an integer by Fermat's Little Theorem, so the first term does not contribute to the parity of  $\left\lfloor \frac{n^k}{k} \right\rfloor$ . Hence we just need  $\left\lfloor \frac{n^{n^z-z}}{p} \right\rfloor$  to be odd. By Bertrand's Postulate, we can pick  $p$  such that  $\frac{n^{n^z-z}}{2} < p < n^{n^z-z}$  (thus making  $p > n$  and  $p \nmid n$ ), and this automatically makes  $\left\lfloor \frac{n^{n^z-z}}{p} \right\rfloor = 1$ . Therefore, with this choice of  $p$ , we get that  $\left \lfloor \frac{n^k}{k} \right\rfloor$  is even. By varying  $z \ge 1$ , we conclude.

Define  $I_{\triangle}$  and  $J_{\triangle}$  to be the incenter and  $P$ -excenter of  $\triangle$ , respectively.

<span style="color:#F9CB9C">**Claim.**</span>  $\triangle PAI_{\triangle PAD} \sim \triangle PJ_{\triangle PAD}D$ *Proof.* Note that  $AI_{\triangle PAD}DJ_{\triangle PAD}$  is cyclic as  $\angle I_{\triangle PAD}AJ_{\triangle} = \angle J_{\triangle PAD}DI_{\triangle} = 90^{\circ}$ . Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\angle PAI_{\triangle PAD} = I_{\triangle PAD}AD = \angle I_{\triangle PAD}J_{\triangle PAD}D.
\end{align*}
As  $\angle APJ_{\triangle PAD} = \angle J_{\triangle PAD}PD$ , then  $\triangle PAI_{\triangle PAD} \sim \triangle PJ_{\triangle PAD}D$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Thus, by similar triangles, then:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{AP}{PI_{\triangle PAD}} = \dfrac{J_{\triangle PAD}P}{PD} \quad \longrightarrow \quad AP \cdot PD = J_{\triangle PAD}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD}.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\[
\begin{cases}
AP \cdot PB &= J_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PBA} 
BP \cdot PC &= J_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PCB} 
CP \cdot PD &= J_{\triangle PDC}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PDC} 
DP \cdot PA &= J_{\triangle PAD}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD}.
\end{cases}
\]
Therefore:
\begin{align*}
AP \cdot BP \cdot CP \cdot PD &= AP \cdot BP \cdot CP \cdot PD 
J_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PBA} \cdot J_{\triangle PDC}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PDC} &= J_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PCB} \cdot J_{\triangle PAD}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD} 
\left(J_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PDC}\right) \left(I_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PDC}\right) &= \left(J_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PAD}\right) \left(I_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD}\right)
\end{align*}
Now, if the incenters are concyclic, then by power of point,  $I_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PDC} = I_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD}$ . So 
\[ J_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PDC} = J_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PAD}, \] which by power of point implies the excenters are concyclic.

Instead, if the excenters are concyclic then  $J_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PDC} = J_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PJ_{\triangle PAD}$ , so
\[ I_{\triangle PBA}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PDC} = I_{\triangle PCB}P \cdot PI_{\triangle PAD}, \]
which implies the incenters are concyclic by power of point. 

Thus, the incenters are concyclic if and only if the excenters are concyclic, as desired.

