Consider the circumcircle of  $\triangle PQD$ . Now  $X$  must lie on  $\odot(PQD)$  because  $X$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{PQ}$  and external angle bisector of  $\angle PDQ$ . This as a matter of fact would give us  $\angle PQR = 90^\circ$ . Finally, due the the inscribed angle theorem we are done since  $\angle ARC = 135^\circ$ .  $\blacksquare$ <details><summary>Personal Comments</summary>I don't think this is an easy problem at all for INMO standards also. The external angle bisector is kind of hard to find though experienced contestants surely know this stuff. Overall this was a fine problem and a very neat result.</details>

Let  $R$  denote the circumcenter of  $(PIQ)$ . Then we make the following claim with the observation that  $\angle PDQ = \angle PDB + \angle BDQ = 45^{\circ} + 45^{\circ} = 90^{\circ}$ :**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $PDRQ$  is cyclic.
*Proof:* We find that 
\begin{align*}
\angle PRQ &= 360^{\circ} - 2 \angle PIQ 
&= 360^{\circ} - 2 \angle AIC 
&= 360^{\circ} - 2 \left(90^{\circ} + \frac{1}{2}(90^{\circ})\right) 
&= 90^{\circ} 
&= \angle PDQ. \square
\end{align*}

Hence we find that \[\angle CDQ = 45^{\circ} = \angle RPQ = \angle RDQ, \] so that  $D, R, C$  are collinear and consequently  $R$  lies on  $\overline{AC}$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

