Indeed let us invert at  $M$ , let  $A,B,C,N,P, K \mapsto A',B',C', N', P', K'$  respectively. Let the circumcircle of  $\displaystyle \triangle A'MC'$  be called  $\omega$ . Then  $K'$  is the center of  $\omega$ ,  $MB'$  intersects  $\omega$  for the second time at  $N'$ , and  $P'$  is a point on  $\omega$  such that  $M,P',B',K'$  are concyclic. 

The angle condition translates to  $\angle A'MP' = \angle C'MN'$ , which implies that  $A'C' \parallel N'P'$  since we get an isosceles trapezium  $A'C'P'N'$ .

Note that  $A'B' = B'C'$  is the same as proving that  $B'$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $A'C'$  which happens if and only if  $B'$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $P'N'$ . This is easy to see since   $$ \angle M'B'P' = 180^\circ - \angle MK'P' = 180^\circ - 2 \cdot \angle BN'P' \implies \angle BN'P' = \angle BP'N'. \quad \square $$

