*Proof.* Consider a vertex  $v_i \in \mathcal{C}$ . Let the neighbors of  $v_i$  in  $\mathcal{C}$  be  $v_{i + 1}$  and  $v_{i - 1}$ . By the definition of **convex hull**, since  $v_i \in \mathcal{C}$ , we can choose a line  $\ell$  passing through  $v_i$  such that  $v_{i - 1}$  and  $v_{i + 1}$  lies on the same side of line  $\ell$ .   We'll consider the following diagram.
